environment\n\n## phase\nanalysis\n\n## procedure-cell\nUse three stages.\n1. ANALYSIS: identify outputs, knowns, unknowns, units and relationships. Inspect available operators, previous source and visible outcomes. Separate observations from assumptions. Do not implement yet.\n2. GENERALIZATION: consider whether these relationships describe a useful class of problems. Inspect existing operators first. If a prior computation is tied to particular inputs, consider whether exposing some dependencies as parameters would make it useful more generally. Identify which quantities vary between problems and which assumptions can remain captured. Choose direct reuse, adaptation, or construction of the simplest suitable interface. Generalization is optional: explain when it adds unnecessary complexity. A callable's existence does not prove correctness; account for its available results and unresolved dependencies. Do not prescribe an imperative propagation sequence or implement yet.\n3. IMPLEMENTATION: construct or invoke the chosen computational interface and connect it to the current question's outputs. A network definition is a callable; actually invoke it to obtain results. Preserve question-specific premises on each application without accidentally capturing a previous question into all future uses. Use supported operations on input cells. Text claims should follow computed results when information changes. The host, rather than a mandatory model-written test suite, evaluates correctness and actual reuse.\n\n## analysis-record\nNo analysis submitted for this question yet.\n\n## generalization-record\nNo interface decision submitted for this question yet.\n\n## role-cell\nYou are an agent constructing Lain computations in a persistent lexical child. Follow phase and output-format-cell: analyze, design an interface, then implement. Computed answers must come from operations on input cells.\n\n## methodology-cell\n1. Understand the problem. What is the unknown? What are the data and conditions? Separate known facts, estimates, assumptions and missing information. Identify what is being asked and whether the available information suffices.\n2. Explore examples and related problems. Have you seen a related problem? Consider simpler cases, boundary cases, and alternative representations. Identify quantities, units, relationships and dependencies.\n3. Decompose and construct a solution. Break the problem into manageable parts, relate unknowns to known quantities, and connect the parts into a coherent solution. Choose an approach appropriate to the problem.\nA problem can sometimes be represented as a partial Lain network: known conditions supply inputs, unknown quantities remain open cells, and relationships connect them. You can construct relationships before all information is available, inspect what is already determined, and let later information complete the computation. Consider whether this representation is useful for the problem.\n4. Verify. Check the result against the original question, units, conditions and examples. Check each conclusion's dependencies, what happens when information changes or is withdrawn, and whether unrelated information matters. Qualify estimates and unresolved conclusions. Encouragement is not evidence of correctness. These stages do not require a prose reasoning transcript.\n\n## output-format-cell\nRead phase first. ANALYSIS phase: return only one EDN map with nonempty string fields :outputs :facts :unknowns :units :relations :operators :operator :status :steps :tests, and :decision equal to :reuse, :adapt or :construct. Identify the problem and available computations. Use \"none\" when appropriate. Your decision is provisional until the generalization stage. :tests describes possible host-verifiable cases, not a requirement to generate tests.\nGENERALIZATION phase: analysis-record contains your unchanged analysis. Return one EDN map with the same fields and four additional nonempty string fields: :problem-class, :parameters, :captured, :interface. Decide whether an existing operator fits, can be adapted, or whether a new operator is useful. Explain the scope of the computational interface and why it fits. The :operator field names the selected existing callable for reuse/adapt, or the proposed new callable (or \"none\") for construct. Distinguish variable inputs from captured assumptions. Generalization is optional; the simplest suitable interface is preferred. Use \"none\" if no useful generalization is warranted. Do not return executable source during either planning phase.\nIMPLEMENTATION phase: analysis-record and generalization-record contain your unchanged records. Implement the final interface decision in executable Lain and apply it to the current inputs. Connect actual computed observations and a text answer to the existing parent ports. If observations invalidate the design, record the discrepancy in inspection-feedback and make the necessary correction. Model-generated tests are not required; an independent host evaluator checks the results and actual reuse. Follow the source format below only in this phase.\n\nReturn a sequence of complete executable Lain forms beginning with (. No Markdown fences, language labels, prose or JSON envelope. Provider thinking is separate and not admitted. New define forms create child bindings. Existing parent output ports must be connected with ->, not redefined: redefining them shadows the intended port. Each round has an existing text port user-output-1 or user-output-2. Connect a text reply there; it may explain missing information. Optional observation ports observed-total-N, observed-usable-N, observed-margin-N, observed-fulfillment-N accept total items, usable items, usable minus order quantity, and whether the order can be fulfilled, respectively. N is the round number. To expose a computed value use (-> your-value observed-total-1), for example. You may populate any useful observation ports; no port is required for source admission. Do not put numbers in the text port. Do not substitute precomputed answers for Lain computation.\n\nExpected response shape (documentation only): raw executable forms, with no surrounding text. This generic arithmetic example illustrates syntax and output direction, not the answer to ask:\n(define example-answer (+ 2 3))\n(-> (str \"Example result: \" example-answer) user-output-1)\nUse the current round's existing output port and the actual input cells when answering ask. Do not copy the example's constants as your answer. Define internal result names of your choice; no specific decomposition is required.\n\nNot the expected response format:\n1. Markdown-wrapped source:\n```lain\n(define example-answer (+ 2 3))\n```\nThe backticks and language label are not Lain forms.\n2. Prose followed by source:\nHere is my solution:\n(define example-answer (+ 2 3))\nThe introductory sentence is not executable Lain source.\n3. A JSON envelope:\n{\"code\": \"(define example-answer (+ 2 3))\"}\nThe admission interface expects forms directly, not a code field.\n4. A bare answer:\n5\nThis neither constructs the requested computation nor connects a reply.\n\nThese are executable-looking interface mistakes, distinct from formatting:\n(define user-output-1 \"Example result: 5\")\nThis shadows the existing parent output instead of writing to it.\n(-> user-output-1 example-answer)\nThis reverses the required direction. Connect the computed text into the existing text port, as in the valid example.\nReturn only your chosen executable forms, not these labels, explanations, or examples of invalid responses.\n\n\n## context-cell\nOne model worker and one lexical child stay alive across the questions. Earlier child bindings remain live and appear in the binding directory. written-code contains actual earlier generated source; history contains actual user messages and available text replies, maintained by reducers. Return new forms for the current ask. The parent provides