ception/message and exception/data. Empty error evidence does not prove correctness. This adapter reads merge-error evidence; it does not catch every compiler or scheduler exception.\nadmission-status is the latest source admission receipt. A rejected receipt is an admission failure; inspect its diagnostic and form identity. Successful earlier forms remain installed. source admission is not proof that every output computed.\n\nLiteral examples below are documentation, not networks already installed in your environment. Names beginning demo- belong only to the examples.\n\nExample 1: intentional partial computation.\n(define demo-input)\n(define demo-ready (+ 2 3))\n(define demo-answer (+ demo-input demo-ready))\n(define demo-view (inspect:cell (cell:reference demo-answer)))\nBefore demo-input is supplied, demo-ready is 5 and demo-answer is unknown. In a later extension of the SAME child, (-> 7 demo-input) makes demo-answer 12. No recompilation or model evaluation is needed to complete the already constructed relationship. This does not make + automatically solve backwards.\n\nExample 2: an unresolved callable, not a syntax error.\n(define demo-later)\n(define demo-waiting (demo-later 4))\n(define demo-directory (environment:overview current-environment (seq:empty)))\nThe directory shows demo-later unknown; demo-waiting has no result. The source uses demo-later in operator position. In a later extension, (-> (network [x] (+ x 1)) demo-later) supplies the missing callable and the existing demo-waiting becomes 5. Do not redefine a parent output port while trying to repair a connection.\n\nExample 3: correct output direction and explicit feedback.\n(define demo-output)\n(-> demo-ready demo-output)\n(-> (inspect:cell (cell:reference demo-output)) inspection-feedback)\nThe first argument of -> supplies information to the second. The feedback port retains the inspection record for a subsequent environment description. Merely defining a computed value does not connect it to an unrelated answer port. The examples do not schedule model calls.\n\nError interpretation:\nAn incomplete reader form such as the literal text (define demo-broken causes source rejection before that form can compile. A runtime merge exception instead yields a contradiction with error evidence describing the operation and operands that failed. Conflicting contributions can also produce a contradiction without an exception record. A blocked output may be intentional missing input, unresolved operator, or incomplete wiring; follow its input connections and inspect the relevant source before claiming a cause. Source references identify places to investigate, not necessarily causes. Never treat an unknown-to-unknown transition as proof of successful withdrawal or repair.\n\n\n## admission-status\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## inspection-feedback\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## metadata-cell\nNames ending in -1 and -2 belong to separate shipments and questions. boxes-N, items-per-box-N, damage-percent-N and order-quantity-N are independently supported input facts; percentage units are 0 through 100. damage-percent-1 is initially unknown; a later published value for it is an estimate. damage-percent-2 is an inspection result. question-N is the premise identifying the corresponding user question and should support conclusions answering it. shared-rule is the shared premise identifying validity of agent-generated mathematical rules; it does not validate the formulas. delivery-note is unrelated metadata. Damage rates can be estimated or inspected as described by the question or supplied status. Do not invent missing facts.\n\n## ask\nA shipment contains 20 boxes of 12 items. We need to fulfill an order for 200 items. The damaged percentage has not been reported yet. What can we determine now, and what remains unresolved about the usable stock and whether we can fulfill the order?\n\n## written-code\n[]\n\n## user-output-1\nState: unknown. unknown\n\n## observed-total-1\nSta