stances; the propagator's natural dataflow already generalizes the partial-computation behavior.\"}", :thinking "The phase is \"generalization\". I need to return one EDN map with the same fields as the analysis-record plus four additional fields: :problem-class, :parameters, :captured, :interface.\n\nLet me analyze what's happening:\n\n1. The analysis-record already identifies:\n   - total = boxes * items-per-box (computable now: 20*12=240)\n   - usable = total * (1 - damage-percent/100) (blocked on damage-percent-1)\n   - margin = usable - order-quantity (blocked)\n   - fulfillment = usable >= order-quantity (blocked)\n\n2. For generalization, I need to consider:\n   - Is there an existing operator that fits? No, there's no pre-built \"shipment fulfillment\" operator.\n   - Can I adapt something? The relationships are simple arithmetic.\n   - Should I construct a new interface? A network that takes boxes, items-per-box, damage-percent, and order-quantity as parameters would be a natural generalization.\n\nThe problem class: \"shipment fulfillment check\" - given a shipment's box count, items per box, damage percentage, and an order quantity, determine total items, usable items, margin, and fulfillment status.\n\nParameters that vary between problems: boxes, items-per-box, damage-percent, order-quantity. These are the variable inputs.\n\nCaptured assumptions: The formula structure (total = boxes * items-per-box, usable = total * (1 - damage/100), margin = usable - order-quantity, fulfillment = usable >= order-quantity). These are the mathematical relationships that remain constant.\n\nInterface: A network taking [boxes, items-per-box, damage-percent, order-quantity] and returning total, usable, margin, fulfillment. This is simple enough that a single network is the right choice. No existing operator fits. The generalization is minimal but useful because the same arithmetic pattern applies to any shipment/order scenario.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether genera